ptimize by storing only last index and reuse previous tuple? Actually tuples are immutable, so we would create new tuple each update. That could be heavy.\n\nAnother approach: Since we break ties first by fewer tokens, and then lexicographic source indices, we can do a DP that returns the best solution for each weight, and then after we find best weight, we reconstruct by iterating items in reverse order, but with a tie-breaking rule: when two choices lead to same score, we want the one that picks earlier indices. In the reconstruction from DP table, we can check at each step whether the current item could be included (score matches dp[weight] - dp[weight-len] == score), and if there are multiple possibilities, we choose the smallest index? Actually, for a given weight, the DP value is max sum. When reconstructing, we need to know which item was included. Typically we store a boolean table 'choice' indicating whether item i was included for that weight. But we can store a single digit: which index was used to achieve that dp[weight]. That is, for each weight, store the last item included (or -1 if none). Then we can reconstruct by following predecessors. This stores only O(budget) integers. To handle lexicographic tie-breaking, when updating dp[weight] with a new item i, if dp[weight] < new_score, replace. If equal, we need to compare the reconstructed sequences. We could reconstruct sequences on the fly using recursion/memoization to compare. That might be heavy but manageable if budget small. Or we can store for each weight the best sequence encoded as a tuple of indices (or a linked list). That is simpler.\n\nGiven typical constraints in these exercises, number of items is small (like <20). So storing full tuples is fine. Let's implement DP that for each weight stores a tuple (total_score, tokens_used, indices_tuple). Actually tokens_used is the weight itself, so we don't need to store separately. We'll store (total_score, indices_tuple) and weight is implicit key. When updating, compare by score, then by indices_tuple lexicographically (smaller tuple preferred). Also note that 0/1 knapsack: we include each item at most once. We'll initialize dp as list of None for weight 0..budget. dp[0] = (0.0, ()) for empty selection. Then for each item i with len L and score S, iterate w from budget down to L: if dp[w-L] is not None, candidate_score = dp[w-L][0] + S, candidate_indices = dp[w-L][1] + (i,). Then compare with dp[w]. If dp[w] is None, set. Else compare: if candidate_score > dp[w][0], set; elif candidate_score == dp[w][0]: then compare candidate_indices lexicographically with dp[w][1]; if candidate_indices < dp[w][1], set. Finally, after processing all items, iterate weight from 0 to budget to find the best: compare total_score, then weight (smaller weight is better for equal score? The problem says \"Break equal utility by fewer used tokens\". So for same total_score, we prefer smaller tokens_used (weight). So after we have dp for each weight, we find the one with max score, then min weight, then lexicographically smallest indices. We'll iterate weight from 0 to budget and keep best according to (score, -weight, indices) but careful: higher score better, then smaller weight better, then smaller indices tuple. Since indices tuple comparison is lexicographic by default. So we can compare tuples: (-score, weight, indices_tuple) and take min. But score is float, weight int, indices tuple. So we can do best = None, then for each weight, if dp[w] is not None: candidate = ( -dp[w][0], w, dp[w][1] ); if best is None or candidate < best: best = candidate. At end, best_indices = list(best[2]), best_tokens = best[1], best_score = -best[0]. Return dict with selected_indices, tokens_used, total_score.\n\nCheck tie-breaking: Since we prefer smaller weight when score equal, and then lexicographic indices, our tuple (-score, weight, indices) works because Python compares left to right: first -score (more negative is larger score), so higher score gives smaller -score (more negative) which is better. Then weight: smaller weight is better, so that fits (since second element, smaller is better). Then indices: smaller tuple is better lexicographically, so that also fits. So taking min works.\n\nEdge cases: Empty selection? budget might be 0 or lengths all > budget, then only selection is empty. Should we allow empty? The problem says \"Select and reconstruct context-scored sentences under an exact token budget\" but doesn't say must select at least one. So empty selection is valid: selected_indices=[], tokens_used=0, total_score=0. However, the DP as above: dp[0] = (0, ()). That works.\n\nAlso note: indices are 0-based. Return in increasing order. Our reconstruction tuple is naturally in insertion order because we append i when we include an item, and we process items in order 0..n-1, so the indices tuple will be sorted automatically because we only add i after all smaller indices? Actually in the DP, when we include item i, we take the previous sequence (which contains indices < i) and append i, so the tuple remains sorted. So it's fine.\n\nNow implement solve(case) accordingly.\n\nMake sure to use only standard library, no imports except maybe typing? Not needed.\n\nReturn JSON-serializable object, so dict with keys selected_indices, tokens_used, total_score. selected_indices list of ints, tokens_used int, total_score float.\n\nNow write the Python code. We'll include in implementation string.\n\nWe must output exactly one JSON object with key \"implementation\" and value the Python source as a JSON string (escaped). So need to escape newlines, quotes, backslashes. I'll write the Python code as a multi-line string and then escape in JSON.\n\nWe'll produce the final JSON output.\n\nMake sure to validate that case has required fields: lengths, scores, budget. But not needed? The evaluator will provide payload with those fields.\n\nNow write code."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"promp